[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue and marked known: the parser reads dates month
first, the fix is queued. The desk's own cause rules (`causes.ts`) say the merged reports hold
three problems. The queued fix closes one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Three, and two of them have no ticket of their own.
</Callout>

## What the 4471 reports hold

<SplitTable ticket={4471} />

## The three problems

1. **Parser.** The shared parser reads every date month first. A day-first tenant's dates come out
   flipped, so 7 April arrives as 4 July. This is the problem #4471 describes, and the queued fix
   covers it.
2. **Form hint.** Through booking-v3, people type what the hint under the date field tells them,
   month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way. A parser fix does not touch this. The fix
   is the hint text, or reading the field as the hint says.
3. **One-day move.** The desk's own notes (`desk-notes.json`) read some reports as a move by one
   day, not a swap. Their words point at time zones: one meeting reached Lisbon on Tuesday and
   Toronto on Monday, one invite arrived before the booking existed, one tenant sees it only in
   June. This reading is mine, from a few reports, so treat it as a lead. It needs an engineer to
   reproduce it.

Each problem now has its own issue in [xpacz/kb-4471-split](https://github.com/xpacz/kb-4471-split/issues).

## Why one ticket held three

- The desk merges on the subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
  All three problems arrive under the same few subjects, so the merge could not tell them apart.
- Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser. That is a guess, and for the form-hint
  reports it is wrong about as often as it is right. The cause depends on the tenant: where they
  are and which booking form they run.

## Lessons

- A ticket's count says how often people hit a problem, not how many problems there are.
  Check the cause before you merge, not only the subject.
- Merge by cause (`sameCause` in `causes.ts`), not by subject.

